  "query_preview": "..\nIssued at 200 PM EST Mon Nov 2 2026\n\nRest of Today-Tonight...Heavy to extreme rainfall has occurred\nover portions of the coastline, with multiple communities\ninundated. The mid-level disturbance is forecast to weaken into \ntonight, providing some relief. However, persistent, heavy \nshowers and storms remain a threat through the afternoon hours \nalong the Treasure Coast. As we transition into the evening and \novernight, PoPs are forecast to decrease, but scattered, onshore \nmoving showers (PoPs up to 50%), will remain possible. Any \nadditional rainfall will inhibit the ability for water to drain, \nso have continued the Flood Watch for Okeechobee and the coastal \ncounties through the overnight hours. Wave heights appear to have \nlingered higher than previously forecast, so have extended the \nHigh Surf Advisory into tonight.\n\nTuesday-Wednesday...The disturbance that has produced heavy\nrainfall over portions of the area will wash out through mid-week,\ndiminishing the overall threat. However, high PWATs 1.7-2\" linger\nover the area through Wednesday, with an upper level disturbance\nover the western Atlantic. Therefore, scattered showers and a few\nstorms remain forecast, especially Tuesday (PoPs 30-60%). While \nadditional rainfall totals of 1-2\" are possible, any additional \nrainfall could exacerbate ongoing, lingering flooding. Thus, the \nFlood Watch has been extended through 10 AM for the coast and \nOkeechobee County. Continued breezy onshore winds Tuesday, with\ngusts 20-25 mph, will slacken into Wednesday, as the pressure\ngradient slackens. High temperatures will remain warm, in the \nlower to mid-80s, while onshore flow keeps low temperatures in the\nupper 60s to lower 70s.\n\nThursday-Sunday...A series of upper level shortwaves look to\ntransit the area late week and into this weekend. While\ntemperature changes will be limited, drier air (nearer to normal\nPWATs) will filter into the area, providing a welcome break from\nthe higher coverage of showers. While some rain will be possible,\ncurrent PoPs are limited to around 20% or less. Lighter winds\nbecome NNW through the weekend, helping to give some relief to the\npersistent onshore flow. Temperatures are forecast to remain \nwarm, only decreasing to near normal (lower 80s) during the \ndaytime hours. Overnight lows fall slightly, but remain in the 60s\nfor most areas. A stronger, drier front will approach the area \nearly next week.",
